`drainSocket()` logs every received chunk via `console.log`, which will pollute test output and slow CI. Also, callers invoke it without awaiting/handling the returned promise, which can lead to unhandled rejections on socket errors. Prefer a quiet drain (`socket.resume()` / `socket.on('data', ...)`) and either `await` it or explicitly `void` it with internal error handling.
```suggestion
function drainSocket(socket: net.Socket): void {
  // Quietly drain the socket to avoid polluting test output and to prevent
  // unhandled promise rejections. Errors while draining are intentionally ignored.
  socket.on('data', () => {
    // discard data
  })
  socket.on('error', () => {
    // ignore errors while draining
  })
  socket.resume()
```

`drainStream` is exported from the package entrypoint (`src/index.ts`) and this change narrows its signature to a single `stream` argument. Previously, internal call sites (and repo tests) passed a context object as the first argument, so this is a breaking change for any external consumers that did the same. Consider keeping a backwards-compatible overload (e.g., accepting `(ctx, stream)` and delegating to `drainStreamInternal`) and deprecating it, or documenting the breaking change and bumping the major version accordingly.
```suggestion
/** @deprecated Use `drainStream(stream)` without a context argument instead. */
export function drainStream(ctx: Context, stream: Stream.Readable): Promise<void>
export function drainStream(stream: Stream.Readable): Promise<void>
export async function drainStream(
  ctxOrStream: Context | Stream.Readable,
  maybeStream?: Stream.Readable,
): Promise<void> {
  const stream =
    typeof (ctxOrStream as any)?.on === 'function' &&
    typeof (ctxOrStream as any)?.read === 'function'
      ? (ctxOrStream as Stream.Readable)
      : (maybeStream as Stream.Readable)
```

This test claims to cover the "already closed" case, but it only verifies a local `closed` flag and then consumes the stream to completion. It does not assert that `readable.closed` is true, nor does it actually exercise the `if (stream.closed)` branch in `drainStream`. Consider either renaming the test to "already ended" (since that’s what it’s testing) or explicitly closing/destroying the stream and asserting `readable.closed === true` before calling `drainStream`.
```suggestion
    let ended = false
    const readable = new stream.Readable({
      read() {
        this.push('data')
        this.push(null) // end the stream
        ended = true
      },
    })

    expect(ended).toBe(false)
    for await (const _ of readable) {
      // consume the stream
    }
    expect(ended).toBe(true)

    // explicitly close the stream so that readable.closed === true
    const waitForClose = new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
      if (readable.closed) {
        resolve()
      } else {
        readable.once('close', () => resolve())
        readable.destroy()
      }
    })
    await waitForClose
    expect(readable.closed).toBe(true)
```

