The version bump logic assumes the current version is strictly `x.y.z`. If `packages/client-common/package.json` ever contains a prerelease/build suffix (e.g. `1.2.3-rc.1`), `split('.').map(Number)` will produce `NaN` and generate an invalid version string. Consider validating `CURRENT` matches `^\d+\.\d+\.\d+$` before computing `NEW` (and fail fast with a clear error) or using a semver-aware bump implementation.
```suggestion
            const current = process.env.CURRENT;
            if (!/^\d+\.\d+\.\d+$/.test(current)) {
              console.error(
                'Unsupported version format in packages/client-common/package.json: ' +
                current +
                '. Expected x.y.z.'
              );
              process.exit(1);
            }
            const [major, minor, patch] = current.split('.').map(Number);
            if (process.env.BUMP_TYPE === 'major') process.stdout.write((major + 1) + '.0.0');
            else if (process.env.BUMP_TYPE === 'minor') process.stdout.write(major + '.' + (minor + 1) + '.0');
            else process.stdout.write(major + '.' + minor + '.' + (patch + 1));
```

